Z||000004||fierospeeder||03-04-2001||12:29 AM||||If you want to do it the real way for storing a car.  You would use 4 jacks and place it underneath the car where a strong part of the frame, or cradle is.<P>What is in the picture is same as the car being on the ground because the blocks are acting as the same force.<P><BR>Those blocks are not strong at all.  You can drop them on asphalt and they will break into pieces.  They are also used in those commercials with the guy with strong abs.<P>They take one of those blocks and the guy has it on his abs and another guy takes a sledge hammer and breaks it into pieces.  No pain at all because physics takes a play in it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||KRMFiero||03-04-2001||01:18 AM||||When i put my fiero on blocks during the winter,  took the tires off and put one block under each A frame, it worked<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (Red)<BR>1985 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (White)<P>     <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/sig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||03-05-2001||07:47 PM||||When I put my car on blocks, I use the hollow semi-hard rubber toy ones.  Much safer, because the car just squashes them and the car never leaves the ground.  W-s-s-s-s-s-t!<P>Okay - for what it's worth, if I want my car to be off the ground like in the above photo, I use a set of 4 cast aluminum VW custom wheels I happen to have.  I jack up one side of the car, lay a VW wheel flat on the ground underneath each of the (2) raised tires and lower the car.  Then raise the other side of the car and do the other side.<P>So, replace the blocks you see in the picture  with wheels lying flat on their faces, and you'd be looking at the way I do it.  Imagine holding a car up with 4 wheels??  Of course, they aren't loaded the way they're used to, but it seems to work fine.  And because the tires sink into the "dish" of the wheels, there's no way they're going anywhere.          ||||reg||12
